{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0162AXMwUqQ5jatZyXuGFdJc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Problem\n2\t\n3\tClass: core\n4\t\n5\tThe operator ruled on 2026-09-29 that the waves are built by the product: priority order, plus opportunistic work when a lower-priority issue can take an open claim. The product now holds what the waves need. The plan's order is in the store (#264), the tenant's issues come through `[forge].issues_command` (#267), and work in flight comes from git (`attavo claims`, #251). Nothing puts them together. The frontier is still computed by `frontier.py`, session tooling outside the product with its own overlap rule, and the waves in the Plan Map were drawn by hand.\n6\t\n7\tThis item adds `attavo frontier [--json]`. It makes three reads and writes nothing.\n8\t\n9\t- **Order.** The order comes from the plan's latest version: epic rank, then issue rank within the epic. An open issue the plan does not place is Backlog, last, by number, so a script-filed issue is always somewhere. The output says which order it used.\n10\t- **Wave 0** is the work holding claims now: every branch not on the default branch, with the paths it declares and changes, as `attavo claims` reads them. An item branch also says where it stands, read from the records it carries: spec pushed, spec refused, awaiting approval, approved, building, or in review. The spec record's status is read through `records._load_record`, the one place that parses a record.\n11\t- **Wave 1** walks the order and places each open issue that meets all four conditions:\n12\t  - its predecessors, its `depends` and `ruled` edges, are closed or placed in an earlier wave;\n13\t  - its Claims overlap nothing already placed in the wave;\n14\t  - its Claims overlap nothing still held in wave 0;\n15\t  - under strict opportunism (ruled 2026-09-29), its Claims overlap nothing that an open issue ranked above it claims and that is not yet placed, whether that issue is ready or not. A lower-ranked issue never takes a path a higher-ranked one wants, and it does take a slot on paths nobody above it wants.\n16\t- **Wave k+1** walks what is left as if the earlier waves had merged. When the work in flight blocks everything, wave 1 is empty and wave 2 reads past it.\n17\t- **Overlap** is `attavo.claims.overlap`, the product's only rule.\n18\t- **Not placed.** An issue with no Claims is not placed, because nothing shows it disjoint from anything; neither is an issue whose predecessors never are. Each is listed with its reason.\n19\t- **Why lines.** Every placed issue carries one line per reason it waited: `waits on #281 (depends)`, `waits on branch lane/9-other: y/four.py`, `shares attavo/cli.py with #264 in wave 1`, `yields shared.py to #2, ranked above`. An issue in wave 1 says nothing.\n20\t- **Epic progress** is closed issues over all issues under the epic, as the forge reports them.\n21\t- **No dates.** The output carries no date or duration, because waves are an order, not a forecast.\n22\t- **Refusals.** No plan in the store, or no issues command, is \"not read\" with exit 2. An unset `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` refuses by name, as `claims` does.\n23\t\n24\tEach issue also carries its class. `attavo/issues.py` reads the class from the body's `Class:` line, using the spec's own `CLASS_LINE` and `CLASSES`, or from a single `class:core` or `class:fix` label. Otherwise the class is None, the unclassed queue's reading. The screen's Waves view (FV2, #282) renders `attavo frontier --json`. Its shape:\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\t{\"default\", \"plan_version\", \"order\", \"epics\": [{\"id\", \"title\", \"closed\", \"total\"}],\n28\t \"waves\": [[{\"issue\", \"branch\"?, \"title\", \"epic\", \"class\", \"claims\", \"spec_state\", \"why\"}]],\n29\t \"not_placed\": [{\"issue\", \"title\", \"epic\", \"why\"}]}\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\tThe selector maps `attavo/frontier.py` to `tests/test_frontier.py`, and `attavo/issues.py` now also to that file.\n33\t\n34\tThe stub under `stub/` is the change on `7a5cbfb`: `attavo/frontier.py`, the class read in `attavo/issues.py`, `attavo/cli.py` (a usage line, a help paragraph, dispatch, and `frontier` in the unknown-subcommand list, which keeps its `stops, test` ending), the selector, nine tests in `tests/test_frontier.py` and one in `tests/test_issues.py`. All are red on `7a5cbfb`. `python3 -m mypy` exits 0. The suite passes on it, except four doctor proofs that read this repository's own history, which a scratch copy cannot supply; they fail identically on `7a5cbfb` there. None of the paths is a claim of the operator's re-entry run.\n35\t\n36\tRetires: `frontier.py` (session tooling) and its second overlap rule; waves drawn by hand; the \"Next\" lists in session records as the source of order.\n37\t\n38\t## Claims\n39\t\n40\t- attavo/frontier.py\n41\t- attavo/issues.py\n42\t- attavo/cli.py\n43\t- .attavo/bin/select-tests\n44\t- tests/test_frontier.py\n45\t- tests/test_issues.py\n46\t\n47\t## Criteria\n48\t\n49\t- the-frontier-builds-waves-from-the-plan-the-issues-and-git.sh: on the fixture tenant with an issues command, `attavo frontier --json` is \"not read: no plan in the store\" before a plan is put. Once a plan is put and a lane branch changes `y/four.py`: wave 0 is that branch; #1 is in wave 1, #2 (which depends on #1) in wave 2, #4 in wave 2 with the why \"waits on branch lane/9-other: y/four.py\", and #3 in wave 3 with \"yields shared.py to #2, ranked above\"; #6 (no Claims) is not placed; epic progress reads E1 one of three and Backlog none of three; the plan version and the order are named; `git status --porcelain` is empty. `tests/test_frontier.py` carries the nine tests named and `tests/test_issues.py` the class test.\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4588db83-1ef4-4bee-8419-3aef77f32fac","uuid":"0827c51c-9686-40fd-8a74-3a86d53b77c1","timestamp":"2026-09-30T02:29:21.277Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-281/attest/specs/281/spec.md","content":"## Problem\n\nClass: core\n\nThe operator ruled on 2026-09-29 that the waves are built by the product: priority order, plus opportunistic work when a lower-priority issue can take an open claim. The product now holds what the waves need. The plan's order is in the store (#264), the tenant's issues come through `[forge].issues_command` (#267), and work in flight comes from git (`attavo claims`, #251). Nothing puts them together. The frontier is still computed by `frontier.py`, session tooling outside the product with its own overlap rule, and the waves in the Plan Map were drawn by hand.\n\nThis item adds `attavo frontier [--json]`. It makes three reads and writes nothing.\n\n- **Order.** The order comes from the plan's latest version: epic rank, then issue rank within the epic. An open issue the plan does not place is Backlog, last, by number, so a script-filed issue is always somewhere. The output says which order it used.\n- **Wave 0** is the work holding claims now: every branch not on the default branch, with the paths it declares and changes, as `attavo claims` reads them. An item branch also says where it stands, read from the records it carries: spec pushed, spec refused, awaiting approval, approved, building, or in review. The spec record's status is read through `records._load_record`, the one place that parses a record.\n- **Wave 1** walks the order and places each open issue that meets all four conditions:\n  - its predecessors, its `depends` and `ruled` edges, are closed or placed in an earlier wave;\n  - its Claims overlap nothing already placed in the wave;\n  - its Claims overlap nothing still held in wave 0;\n  - under strict opportunism (ruled 2026-09-29), its Claims overlap nothing that an open issue ranked above it claims and that is not yet placed, whether that issue is ready or not. A lower-ranked issue never takes a path a higher-ranked one wants, and it does take a slot on paths nobody above it wants.\n- **Wave k+1** walks what is left as if the earlier waves had merged. When the work in flight blocks everything, wave 1 is empty and wave 2 reads past it.\n- **Overlap** is `attavo.claims.overlap`, the product's only rule.\n- **Not placed.** An issue with no Claims is not placed, because nothing shows it disjoint from anything; neither is an issue whose predecessors never are. Each is listed with its reason.\n- **Why lines.** Every placed issue carries one line per reason it waited: `waits on #281 (depends)`, `waits on branch lane/9-other: y/four.py`, `shares attavo/cli.py with #264 in wave 1`, `yields shared.py to #2, ranked above`. An issue in wave 1 says nothing.\n- **Epic progress** is closed issues over all issues under the epic, as the forge reports them.\n- **No dates.** The output carries no date or duration, because waves are an order, not a forecast.\n- **Refusals.** No plan in the store, or no issues command, is \"not read\" with exit 2. An unset `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` refuses by name, as `claims` does.\n\nEach issue also carries its class. `attavo/issues.py` reads the class from the body's `Class:` line, using the spec's own `CLASS_LINE` and `CLASSES`, or from a single `class:core` or `class:fix` label. Otherwise the class is None, the unclassed queue's reading. The screen's Waves view (FV2, #282) renders `attavo frontier --json`. Its shape:\n\n```\n{\"default\", \"plan_version\", \"order\", \"epics\": [{\"id\", \"title\", \"closed\", \"total\"}],\n \"waves\": [[{\"issue\", \"branch\"?, \"title\", \"epic\", \"class\", \"claims\", \"spec_state\", \"why\"}]],\n \"not_placed\": [{\"issue\", \"title\", \"epic\", \"why\"}]}\n```\n\nThe selector maps `attavo/frontier.py` to `tests/test_frontier.py`, and `attavo/issues.py` now also to that file.\n\nThe stub under `stub/` is the change on `7a5cbfb`: `attavo/frontier.py`, the class read in `attavo/issues.py`, `attavo/cli.py` (a usage line, a help paragraph, dispatch, and `frontier` in the unknown-subcommand list, which keeps its `stops, test` ending), the selector, nine tests in `tests/test_frontier.py` and one in `tests/test_issues.py`. All are red on `7a5cbfb`. `python3 -m mypy` exits 0. The suite passes on it, except four doctor proofs that read this repository's own history, which a scratch copy cannot supply; they fail identically on `7a5cbfb` there. None of the paths is a claim of the operator's re-entry run.\n\nRetires: `frontier.py` (session tooling) and its second overlap rule; waves drawn by hand; the \"Next\" lists in session records as the source of order.\n\n## Claims\n\n- attavo/frontier.py\n- attavo/issues.py\n- attavo/cli.py\n- .attavo/bin/select-tests\n- tests/test_frontier.py\n- tests/test_issues.py\n\n## Criteria\n\n- the-frontier-builds-waves-from-the-plan-the-issues-and-git.sh: on the fixture tenant with an issues command, `attavo frontier --json` is \"not read: no plan in the store\" before a plan is put. Once a plan is put and a lane branch changes `y/four.py`: wave 0 is that branch; #1 is in wave 1, #2 (which depends on #1) in wave 2, #4 in wave 2 with the why \"waits on branch lane/9-other: y/four.py\", and #3 in wave 3 with \"yields shared.py to #2, ranked above\"; #6 (no Claims) is not placed; epic progress reads E1 one of three and Backlog none of three; the plan version and the order are named; `git status --porcelain` is empty. `tests/test_frontier.py` carries the nine tests named and `tests/test_issues.py` the class test.\n","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":50}}}
